# Build-time configuration template.
#
# The real values live in `.env`, which is committed. Vite inlines `import.meta.env`
# when it builds, so these have to exist at build time or the deployed board ships with
# no Supabase connection: the page renders and the timetable works, but there are no
# live tap counts and no requests to Supabase. Nothing errors. `npm run verify:bundle`
# exists to catch that.
#
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
# VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=sb_publishable_...
#
# Optional: dataset the public board reads. Overridable per page with
# ?dataset=some-other-dataset. Defaults to expo-demo when unset.
# VITE_BOARDING_DATASET=expo-demo
#
# Why the publishable key is safe to commit, and the token is not:
#
#   The project URL and the publishable key are public by design. The key replaces
#   the old `anon` JWT, which was equally public and equally scoped by RLS. Both ship
#   in the client bundle either way, so committing them adds no exposure.
#
#   The ingest token is a secret and must never appear in a VITE_ variable or in any
#   committed file. It is typed into the kiosk page's Connection panel and lives only
#   in the booth laptop's localStorage. RLS rejects any insert without it, so if it
#   leaks, anyone can forge taps.
#
# `.env.local` is gitignored and still overrides anything set in `.env`.
# `.env.verify` is committed on purpose and holds fake values for the identity guard.
